WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.307
My name is Jennifer Lippincott-Schwartz

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:09.876
and I work at the National

00:00:09.877 --> 00:00:11.511
Institute of Health.

00:00:11.512 --> 00:00:13.646
Today I'd like to talk about

00:00:13.647 --> 00:00:14.881
the role of lipids

00:00:14.882 --> 00:00:17.383
and cholesterol in the regulation of

00:00:17.384 --> 00:00:20.486
trafficking across the secretory pathway.

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:22.889
Now lipids are a major component

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.490
of the secretory pathway

00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:26.726
so it's important to try to understand

00:00:26.727 --> 00:00:28.628
what roles they play

00:00:28.629 --> 00:00:32.398
other than just as a conveyer

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:33.733
of or a constituent

00:00:33.734 --> 00:00:37.203
of the bilayer that comprises this system.

00:00:37.204 --> 00:00:39.705
Before I get into describing

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:41.941
what the roles of these lipids

00:00:41.942 --> 00:00:43.142
might be in regulating the

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:44.043
secretory pathway

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:45.845
let me first give you a

00:00:45.846 --> 00:00:49.115
perspective of this system.

00:00:49.116 --> 00:00:51.784
The secretory pathway is characteristic

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:54.120
of all eukaryotic cells

00:00:54.121 --> 00:00:55.388
and is comprised

00:00:55.389 --> 00:00:57.890
of three major compartments:

00:00:57.891 --> 00:01:00.326
the endoplasmic reticulum, the Golgi

00:01:00.327 --> 00:01:01.561
and the plasma membrane

00:01:01.562 --> 00:01:03.262
as well as transporting intermediates

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:06.532
that flow between these

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.768
different organelles.

00:01:08.769 --> 00:01:10.670
Newly synthesized proteins that enter

00:01:10.671 --> 00:01:11.838
the secretory pathway

00:01:11.839 --> 00:01:13.539
do so at the level of the

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:14.607
endoplasmic reticulum

00:01:14.608 --> 00:01:16.909
where they get processed and folded

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.411
and then transported

00:01:18.412 --> 00:01:20.880
to the Golgi apparatus through

00:01:20.881 --> 00:01:21.948
small vesicular

00:01:21.949 --> 00:01:26.652
tubular carriers into the Golgi apparatus

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:28.321
where they then meet up

00:01:28.322 --> 00:01:30.022
with a variety of

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:31.624
different enzymes that can process

00:01:31.625 --> 00:01:33.059
these proteins.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.261
And ultimately the proteins then move into

00:01:35.262 --> 00:01:37.663
a different set of carriers

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:39.966
that ferry them to the plasma membrane

00:01:39.967 --> 00:01:43.970
or to other final destinations within the cell.

00:01:43.971 --> 00:01:45.905
Now traditionally people who have studied

00:01:45.906 --> 00:01:50.510
this very dynamic  system

00:01:50.511 --> 00:01:53.379
have focused on the role of proteins

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.983
of this machinery in regulating the trafficking

00:01:56.984 --> 00:01:59.585
between these different compartments.

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:03.389
And important roles for coat proteins,

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:04.524
small GTPases,

00:02:04.525 --> 00:02:07.460
tethering factors and fusion proteins

00:02:07.461 --> 00:02:10.496
have all been found to play important roles

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:13.432
in this process by which molecules

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:16.135
move from one place to another

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:17.537
within the system.

00:02:17.538 --> 00:02:19.505
And this movie is just showing you

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:19.906
an example

00:02:19.907 --> 00:02:21.474
of these dynamic movements,

00:02:21.475 --> 00:02:24.310
where transported intermediates are forming

00:02:24.311 --> 00:02:28.381
of the surface of the ER and are targeting

00:02:28.382 --> 00:02:29.415
to the Golgi apparatus

00:02:29.416 --> 00:02:32.919
found in this central region here

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:37.023
through this very complex protein-based

00:02:37.024 --> 00:02:39.258
machinery.

00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:43.396
Now, less understood is the roles of lipids

00:02:43.397 --> 00:02:44.730
in this process.

00:02:44.731 --> 00:02:46.365
Clearly lipids are important

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:47.366
because they form

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:51.237
the bilayer into which proteins

00:02:51.238 --> 00:02:52.338
are conveyed

00:02:52.339 --> 00:02:54.674
within this secretory system.

00:02:54.675 --> 00:02:57.743
But do lipids help orchestrate

00:02:57.744 --> 00:02:59.745
other features of this system.

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:01.581
Are they regulating

00:03:01.582 --> 00:03:03.149
in a more fundamental way

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.219
the way that the membranes are organized

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.522
and proteins are flowing within this system.

00:03:09.523 --> 00:03:10.790
That's the question

00:03:10.791 --> 00:03:14.660
that I think is very very interesting.

00:03:14.661 --> 00:03:17.263
Now there are three major classes of lipids

00:03:17.264 --> 00:03:19.765
that comprise the secretory pathway.

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:22.435
These include glycerophospholipids,

00:03:22.436 --> 00:03:23.603
sphingolipids

00:03:23.604 --> 00:03:24.904
and cholesterol.

00:03:24.905 --> 00:03:27.473
And these three lipids have very different

00:03:27.474 --> 00:03:28.674
characteristics

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:31.577
when they exist within a bilayer.

00:03:31.578 --> 00:03:35.581
The glycerophospholipids have fatty acid tails

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:41.587
that have a lot of essentially

00:03:41.588 --> 00:03:43.723
unsaturated carbon atoms

00:03:43.724 --> 00:03:46.058
which make them flexible

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:47.860
when they sit in a bilayer

00:03:47.861 --> 00:03:50.730
and as a result the thickness of that bilayer

00:03:50.731 --> 00:03:55.067
is relatively thin relative to bilayers

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:57.503
that are enriched in sphingolipids

00:03:57.504 --> 00:04:00.573
which have longer

00:04:00.574 --> 00:04:04.110
and more saturated fatty acids

00:04:04.111 --> 00:04:06.212
associated with them.

00:04:06.213 --> 00:04:09.215
Now, in the presence of cholesterol

00:04:09.216 --> 00:04:11.150
which is a flat four carbon

00:04:11.151 --> 00:04:14.053
chained sterol molecule,

00:04:14.054 --> 00:04:16.789
the sphingolipids will become

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:18.558
much more tightly packed

00:04:18.559 --> 00:04:21.327
and as a result create a thicker bilayer

00:04:21.328 --> 00:04:22.795
relative to bilayers

00:04:22.796 --> 00:04:24.063
that are enriched in the

00:04:24.064 --> 00:04:26.566
glycerophospholipids.

00:04:26.567 --> 00:04:27.200
Now, one of the things

00:04:27.201 --> 00:04:27.867
that's interesting is

00:04:27.868 --> 00:04:30.903
that when you recombined

00:04:30.904 --> 00:04:32.305
these different lipids

00:04:32.306 --> 00:04:35.174
in an artificial vesicle

00:04:35.175 --> 00:04:38.544
you can see essentially segregation

00:04:38.545 --> 00:04:41.781
of these lipids into different microdomains

00:04:41.782 --> 00:04:42.782
with cholesterol

00:04:42.783 --> 00:04:45.418
and sphingolipids essentially coalescing

00:04:45.419 --> 00:04:47.553
into discrete microdomains

00:04:47.554 --> 00:04:52.158
relative to the phosphoglycerol,

00:04:52.159 --> 00:04:54.293
the glycerophospholipids,

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:56.429
which distribute more uniformly

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.298
within these vesicles.

00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:01.300
Now interestingly, there is,

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:02.768
it's known that there is

00:05:02.769 --> 00:05:04.604
a gradient of lipid composition

00:05:04.605 --> 00:05:07.340
of these three classes of lipids

00:05:07.341 --> 00:05:10.376
across the secretory pathway.

00:05:10.377 --> 00:05:13.246
The ER is primarily enriched

00:05:13.247 --> 00:05:15.882
in glycerophospholipids

00:05:15.883 --> 00:05:17.416
and that is in contrast,

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:19.085
in striking contrast

00:05:19.086 --> 00:05:21.754
to the plasma membrane which is enriched

00:05:21.755 --> 00:05:24.190
in sphingolipids and sterols.

00:05:24.191 --> 00:05:27.994
The Golgi apparatus has a mixed richness.

00:05:27.995 --> 00:05:32.598
So, it’s got an intermediate composition

00:05:32.599 --> 00:05:34.167
between plasma membrane

00:05:34.168 --> 00:05:36.869
and ER-like lipid.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:40.106
Now, this gradient of composition

00:05:40.107 --> 00:05:40.640
of these lipids

00:05:40.641 --> 00:05:44.710
across the secretory pathway

00:05:44.711 --> 00:05:47.013
presumably plays an important role

00:05:47.014 --> 00:05:48.447
in the discrete functions

00:05:48.448 --> 00:05:51.384
of these organelles within the system.

00:05:51.385 --> 00:05:53.986
So, the ER is shown here,

00:05:53.987 --> 00:05:57.423
which is primarily enriched

00:05:57.424 --> 00:05:59.759
in these glycerophospholipids,

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:02.795
has lipids therefore that are loosely

00:06:02.796 --> 00:06:06.199
packed and deformable

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:09.235
which makes them suitable for insertion

00:06:09.236 --> 00:06:11.704
and folding of proteins which is

00:06:11.705 --> 00:06:14.040
the primary function of the endoplasmic

00:06:14.041 --> 00:06:14.907
reticulum vis-a-vis

00:06:14.908 --> 00:06:17.210
the secretory pathway.

00:06:17.211 --> 00:06:19.879
The plasma membrane by contrast

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.081
is enriched in sphingolipids

00:06:22.082 --> 00:06:23.416
and cholesterol.

00:06:23.417 --> 00:06:25.985
This makes this bilayer thicker and

00:06:25.986 --> 00:06:28.988
less permeable to small molecules

00:06:28.989 --> 00:06:31.924
which provides an impermeable barrier

00:06:31.925 --> 00:06:34.327
to the outside and also allows

00:06:34.328 --> 00:06:35.394
the plasma membrane

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:37.563
to be moved and manipulated

00:06:37.564 --> 00:06:41.734
by the cytoskeleton as shown here

00:06:41.735 --> 00:06:43.736
where you are looking at the interaction

00:06:43.737 --> 00:06:45.605
of the actin cytoskeleton

00:06:45.606 --> 00:06:46.839
with the plasma membrane

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:51.377
at the cells leading edge.

00:06:51.378 --> 00:06:53.412
The Golgi apparatus is intermediate

00:06:53.413 --> 00:06:54.647
in concentration

00:06:54.648 --> 00:06:59.118
between these different phospholipids

00:06:59.119 --> 00:07:00.853
and cholesterol.

00:07:00.854 --> 00:07:03.656
And it's function within

00:07:03.657 --> 00:07:04.524
the secretory pathway

00:07:04.525 --> 00:07:08.995
could potentially be optimized as a result

00:07:08.996 --> 00:07:11.497
of that mixed concentration

00:07:11.498 --> 00:07:14.867
because it's situated between the ER

00:07:14.868 --> 00:07:15.902
and the plasma membrane

00:07:15.903 --> 00:07:16.869
and is essentially

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:18.804
where sorting decisions are made

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:19.872
as to whether

00:07:19.873 --> 00:07:22.375
proteins essentially leave

00:07:22.376 --> 00:07:23.676
the ER-Golgi system

00:07:23.677 --> 00:07:27.313
or move on to the plasma membrane.

00:07:27.314 --> 00:07:30.183
So one of the biggest questions in the field

00:07:30.184 --> 00:07:31.918
is how this lipid gradient

00:07:31.919 --> 00:07:33.853
across the secretory pathway

00:07:33.854 --> 00:07:36.422
is generated and maintained.

00:07:36.423 --> 00:07:38.958
I just emphasized how

00:07:38.959 --> 00:07:40.359
this lipid gradient

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.128
can play a very important role

00:07:42.129 --> 00:07:43.095
in the functions

00:07:43.096 --> 00:07:44.797
of these different organelles

00:07:44.798 --> 00:07:46.032
so how this gradient

00:07:46.033 --> 00:07:48.201
is maintained and generated

00:07:48.202 --> 00:07:50.136
is an important question.

00:07:50.137 --> 00:07:51.871
Now, we know that cholesterol

00:07:51.872 --> 00:07:53.639
is synthesized in the ER

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:56.008
but is shipped very efficiently

00:07:56.009 --> 00:07:57.710
out to the Golgi apparatus

00:07:57.711 --> 00:07:59.846
and on the plasma membrane.

00:07:59.847 --> 00:08:02.315
What explains this we don't know.

00:08:02.316 --> 00:08:05.117
One possibility is that proteins

00:08:05.118 --> 00:08:07.887
essentially can partition

00:08:07.888 --> 00:08:10.189
with cholesterol and help

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:11.958
drive that molecule

00:08:11.959 --> 00:08:14.227
into the Golgi apparatus which is where

00:08:14.228 --> 00:08:15.294
the sphingolipids

00:08:15.295 --> 00:08:18.564
are preferentially synthesized.

00:08:18.565 --> 00:08:20.466
The cholesterol and sphingolipids

00:08:20.467 --> 00:08:22.034
could then potentially

00:08:22.035 --> 00:08:24.070
partition with each other

00:08:24.071 --> 00:08:25.338
to drive the movement

00:08:25.339 --> 00:08:28.441
of that population of molecules

00:08:28.442 --> 00:08:30.243
on to the plasma membrane.

00:08:30.244 --> 00:08:32.778
But there are complexities to this system.

00:08:32.779 --> 00:08:34.647
For instance, cholesterol

00:08:34.648 --> 00:08:36.182
is capable of coming out

00:08:36.183 --> 00:08:38.684
of the bilayer altogether and associate

00:08:38.685 --> 00:08:41.220
with lipid-transfer proteins

00:08:41.221 --> 00:08:42.655
which are cytosolic proteins

00:08:42.656 --> 00:08:45.358
that can potentially deliver cholesterol

00:08:45.359 --> 00:08:47.026
throughout this system.

00:08:47.027 --> 00:08:48.394
So, it's not clear how

00:08:48.395 --> 00:08:51.564
cholesterol really could

00:08:51.565 --> 00:08:55.234
be regulated in this system to drive

00:08:55.235 --> 00:08:57.570
these different partitioning events

00:08:57.571 --> 00:09:00.339
if it also has this dynamic component

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:01.240
where it can exchange

00:09:01.241 --> 00:09:02.175
within the cytoplasm.

00:09:02.176 --> 00:09:04.677
So, we need to try to understand

00:09:04.678 --> 00:09:07.613
how all of these lipids are behaving

00:09:07.614 --> 00:09:09.015
within the system.

00:09:09.016 --> 00:09:11.984
Which is going to require ability to

00:09:11.985 --> 00:09:13.753
have better lipid probes

00:09:13.754 --> 00:09:15.821
to monitor these lipids

00:09:15.822 --> 00:09:17.790
as they are floxing and distributing

00:09:17.791 --> 00:09:20.459
within this system as well as

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:23.729
visualizing the composition

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.031
of these different organelles,

00:09:25.032 --> 00:09:27.266
visibly lipids and the proteins

00:09:27.267 --> 00:09:29.468
that are distributed with them.

00:09:29.469 --> 00:09:32.071
Now, another question that

00:09:32.072 --> 00:09:34.941
relates to what I just mentioned

00:09:34.942 --> 00:09:37.143
with regards to that lipid gradient

00:09:37.144 --> 00:09:37.710
is whether

00:09:37.711 --> 00:09:40.146
that gradient itself is playing a role

00:09:40.147 --> 00:09:41.814
in protein sorting and trafficking

00:09:41.815 --> 00:09:43.616
within the secretory pathway.

00:09:43.617 --> 00:09:45.618
And the reason for thinking about this

00:09:45.619 --> 00:09:48.888
is that transmembrane proteins

00:09:48.889 --> 00:09:50.256
favor being

00:09:50.257 --> 00:09:53.893
in a lipid environment that matches

00:09:53.894 --> 00:09:56.162
the length or their transmembrane domain.

00:09:56.163 --> 00:09:57.864
So proteins that have long

00:09:57.865 --> 00:09:59.131
transmembrane domains

00:09:59.132 --> 00:10:02.835
will tend to want to reside

00:10:02.836 --> 00:10:03.769
in a lipid environment

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:05.371
that's enriched in sphingolipids

00:10:05.372 --> 00:10:06.539
and cholesterol,

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:08.841
whereas a protein with a short

00:10:08.842 --> 00:10:10.009
transmembrane domain

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:11.544
will prefer to be in an

00:10:11.545 --> 00:10:13.145
environment enriched

00:10:13.146 --> 00:10:15.081
in these glycerophospholipids.

00:10:15.082 --> 00:10:17.483
So, that let many to hypothesize

00:10:17.484 --> 00:10:19.785
that the way proteins actually

00:10:19.786 --> 00:10:22.522
distribute within the secretory pathway

00:10:22.523 --> 00:10:25.725
is through their moving

00:10:25.726 --> 00:10:28.661
and partitioning to particular lipid

00:10:28.662 --> 00:10:29.095
environments

00:10:29.096 --> 00:10:32.398
that fit their transmembrane domain.

00:10:32.399 --> 00:10:34.100
And consistent with that

00:10:34.101 --> 00:10:35.535
researchers have shown

00:10:35.536 --> 00:10:37.637
that by modifying the length

00:10:37.638 --> 00:10:39.005
of the transmembrane domain

00:10:39.006 --> 00:10:41.674
of a protein you can shift its distribution

00:10:41.675 --> 00:10:44.343
within the secretory pathway.

00:10:44.344 --> 00:10:47.246
Now a third question relates to whether

00:10:47.247 --> 00:10:50.349
this partitioning of the lipids

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:53.719
within this system

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:57.290
can be integrated with protein machinery

00:10:57.291 --> 00:10:59.825
to play a key role in the way

00:10:59.826 --> 00:11:01.127
that this secretory pathway

00:11:01.128 --> 00:11:05.498
is actually acting to sort and traffic protein.

00:11:05.499 --> 00:11:08.067
And here is an incredibly

00:11:08.068 --> 00:11:09.268
I think the most probably

00:11:09.269 --> 00:11:11.971
the most profound questionable,

00:11:11.972 --> 00:11:14.240
we know a lot about this protein machinery

00:11:14.241 --> 00:11:16.609
that's driving secretory trafficking

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:18.277
but we don't know how this machinery

00:11:18.278 --> 00:11:20.213
is actually

00:11:20.214 --> 00:11:23.449
influenced by these lipid gradients

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:24.817
that I just mentioned

00:11:24.818 --> 00:11:28.154
and how that lipid that protein machinery

00:11:28.155 --> 00:11:32.225
can itself potentially impact

00:11:32.226 --> 00:11:34.660
the lipid partitioning and the segregation

00:11:34.661 --> 00:11:38.164
of these lipids across the secretory pathway

00:11:38.165 --> 00:11:40.666
in order to allow this system

00:11:40.667 --> 00:11:44.504
to really operate in a robust fashion

00:11:44.505 --> 00:11:46.873
where newly synthesized proteins

00:11:46.874 --> 00:11:49.408
can very efficiently move to this system

00:11:49.409 --> 00:11:51.677
out to the plasma membrane.

00:11:51.678 --> 00:12:18.205
So, with that I want to thank you.

